  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered severe thunderstorms are forecast to \ndevelop late this afternoon and evening across portions of New \nJersey and eastern Pennsylvania. Damaging wind gusts, large hail, \nand a tornado or two will be possible.\n\nA Severe Thunderstorm Watch has been issued for the entire\nregion until midnight tonight. \n\nThe now stationary front is north of our region now. An approaching \nmid level trough is expected to result in showers and storms \ndeveloping along the front and then progressing southward through \nthe evening. The main timing for this round of showers and storms \nappears to be from 6 PM through midnight. As far as the hazards with \nthe storms, it still appears that the primary hazards are damaging \nwinds and cloud-to-ground lightning, but there is potential also for \nlarge hail, heavy rain, and tornadoes. \n\nSurface based CAPE values above 2000 J/kg will be possible as the \nstorms approach, thanks in part to the breaks in the clouds that we \nhave seen since the earlier line of showers moved south of the \nregion. Effective shear still looks like it will be in the 30 - 40 \nkt range, even well south of the front. However, as mentioned by the \nprevious shift, low level shear is limited for most of the region, \nbut directional shear is sufficient for at least some tornado risk, \nparticularly for central and northern Jersey. All that to say, \ndamaging winds still appear to be the primary threat, with large \nhail and tornadoes less likely, but still a threat. \n\nStorm motions look to be fast enough to limit the flooding threat, \nand there doesn't appear much risk for training storms. If there is \nheavy enough rain in a short amount of time, there could be some \nissues with the more flash flood vulnerable areas, such as highly \nurbanized areas, but that threat is limited.\n\nAs for the heat advisory, heat index values in the urban corridor \nare flirting with 100 degrees, so will keep the advisory going as \nis. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...On Wednesday, a shortwave trough could spark another \nround of showers and storms, but it will be working against a \nrelatively stable boundary layer.\n\nIn the wake of the storms Tuesday Night, the front is expected to \nsink back south of the region by Wednesday morning, leaving our \nregion in the relatively cooler and more stable air mass north of \nthe front. The net result is that our region should be in \nnortheasterly flow, and relatively cloudy conditions for much of the \nday. As a result, surface-based instability is going to be almost \nnon-existent, though model soundings do show elevated instability \nfor parcels above 900 mb. \n\nConsequently, the risk for severe storms is still quite conditional \nand uncertain. Given how sharp of a mid level short wave trough is \napproaching, there will be ample forcing, but it is a question of if \nthe elevated instability will be enough to sustain storms in our \nregion.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Hot and humid conditions are forecast across the \nregion on Thursday. \n\nAfter a cool down on Wednesday for most neighborhoods (5 to 10 \ndegrees lower - less across Delmarva), hot and humid conditions are \nonce again expected across the region on Thursday as the frontal \nboundary lifts north. With the front to the north, high temperatures \nare forecast to climb into the upper 80s to mid 90s for many areas \n(around 80 or low 80s far N and W). These temperatures combined with \ndewpoints in the low to mid 70s will again result in heat indices \nwell into the 90s to low 100s south of I-78, perhaps even as high as \n105 degrees for Delmarva.\n\nAnother Heat Advisory may be needed for portions of the area on\nThursday if this trend continues.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...Unsettled conditions will continue on Thursday with \nthe potential for severe thunderstorms. Scattered thunderstorms are \nagain possible on Friday, mainly from Philadelphia south.\n\nAnother round of severe thunderstorms are possible on Thursday. \nFriday could also experience a few severe storms across Delmarva, \ndepending on how quickly the frontal boundary pushes south.\n\nSPC has a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe thunderstorms \non Thursday from about Wilmington to Wildwood NJ southward and a \nMarginal Risk (level 1) to the north of this. \n\nThe northwest flow pattern will remain in place on Thursday and will \ncontinue to be supportive of all severe threats, including damaging \nwinds, large hail, and a few tornadoes, depending on how all the \nmesoscale details pan out. The threat for flash flooding is \ntypically lower with these patterns, but not zero. Training can \nstill occur and a single locale could have multiple days with heavy \nrainfall.\n\nFriday's SPC's 15% probabilities for severe thunderstorms have \nshifted just south of our Delmarva zones, though there is still some \nuncertainty with this. Our latest forecast has about a 30-40% chance \nof showers and thunderstorms south of about Philadelphia.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 5...The Labor Day Weekend will feature mostly dry \nweather with at or below normal tem",
